\section{Progress persistence}{

Once a reader submits an answer, it is saved in the browser's
\code{localStorage} (keyed by page URL and \code{id}) and restored on the next
visit. Because the default \code{id} is the enclosing \code{{r}} chunk's label,
renaming that chunk (or moving the question into a different one) resets
any saved answers for it --- but editing only the question's wording
does not. Pass \code{id} explicitly to pin it.

\code{localStorage} is written straight to disk, not held only in memory, so
this survives closing and reopening the browser, and restarting the
computer -- confirmed with automated tests
(\code{tests/js/persistence.spec.js}) that fully quit and relaunch a real
browser against the same profile, for both a \verb{file://} tutorial (how
\code{\link[=run_tutorial]{run_tutorial()}} opens one) and one served over HTTP. Two things it does
\emph{not} survive, by browser design rather than anything learnr2 controls:
private/incognito windows (their storage is wiped when the window
closes) and the exact page URL changing -- a tutorial re-rendered to a
different path, or opened from a different server/port, starts fresh.

Every page also gets a "Start Over" button, appended automatically to
the bottom of Quarto's TOC sidebar (nothing to opt into -- it's added by
the same JavaScript that renders \code{\link[=question]{question()}}/\code{\link[=student_info]{student_info()}}, as long
as the tutorial has a sidebar to put it in, i.e. \code{toc: true}). Clicking
it, after a confirmation prompt, clears every \code{question()}/
\code{\link[=student_info]{student_info()}} answer \emph{and} every \code{{webr}} exercise's persisted code
(\code{persist: true}) for this page on this device, then reloads -- a clean
slate, without needing to know that both live under different
\code{localStorage} key prefixes. It deliberately leaves alone the random
per-device id \code{\link[=download_answers_button]{download_answers_button()}} embeds in a submission's
metadata, since that identifies this browser across every tutorial and
visit, not this one tutorial's progress.
}

